WEBVTT

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:17.970
The young genius who 
descended into darkness,

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.740
the one who would rule 
over the underworld,

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.560
Akagi Shigeru, has awakened.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.260
That legendary night. 
Game number eight.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.970
Neither Yagi nor Akagi 
have attempted a win.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.010
It's a draw.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.600
<b>Episode 3</b>

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.600
<b>The Sinister Scheme</b>

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:39.580
Tenpai.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:42.450
<i>Little bastard.</i>

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.450
<i>You threw away your 
mangan win to confirm it?</i>

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:50.500
<i>You wanted to see if you really 
read me incorrectly...</i>

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:54.110
<i>Most people can't do that, 
even though they understand</i>

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:56.010
<i>how useful it could be later in the game.</i>

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.870
<i>This is a game of Mahjong that 
could cost the loser a finger.</i>

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:00.870
<i>Most would try to gain every possible point.</i>

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:02.700
<i>But this kid...</i>

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.270
<i>Doesn't he care what happens to him?</i>

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.370
Yagi-san, would you reveal your hand, please?

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:10.950
You're in tenpai, right?

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.150
Just as I thought.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.120
A twisted way of playing.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.660
Typical of someone who only ever thinks

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.120
about tricking people.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:26.620
So unimaginative.

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:30.360
Yagi...

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:33.560
Don't worry.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.020
I'm fully awake now.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.570
<i>Somewhere deep inside, I still 
wasn't taking this kid seriously.</i>

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:44.580
<i>Well, not anymore.</i>

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:46.670
<i>Why?</i>

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:51.080
<i>Because there's a demon lurking 
right under this kid's skin.</i>

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:56.040
<i>I can't calculate his potential.</i>

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:58.640
<i>Mark my words, this man is</i>

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:00.770
<i>going to become a fearsome 
gambler some day.</i>

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:03.450
<i>But I can still beat him right now.</i>

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.760
<i>Because he doesn't understand 
how kan and riichi really work.</i>

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:07.900
<i>Just wait!</i>

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:12.260
<i>A few more hands, and I'll drain 
the color out of your face.</i>

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:17.010
But it was Akagi who took 
possession of the flow.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:18.010
Tsumo.

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:20.940
In East Hand 2, he easily scores

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.460
1,000 points with a tsumo 
win and picks up speed.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.850
Even if common people are 
able to grasp the flow,

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:27.710
they often can't take advantage of it.

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:31.670
But that's not the case with Akagi.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.920
Mentanpin, tsumo, one dora.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.920
Mentanpin - Combines menzen (closed hand),
tanyao (hand comprised of only 2-8), and pinfu.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:37.820
Mangan.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.660
His hands move without hesitation.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:43.330
He is in harmony with his own rhythm.

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:44.720
Still...

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.340
Yagi is silently waiting for an opportunity.

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:49.650
He's aiming for a counter blow.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:52.680
And then, in South Hand 3...

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:52.680
<b>- South Hand 3 -</b>

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:56.180
The opportunity arises.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:03:59.120
<i>I've been waiting for this hand!</i>

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:03.090
Right now, what Yagi is attempting

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:05.820
is a form of cheating called "The Caterpillar."

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:10.060
He takes two 7 of bamboo 
tiles in his left hand.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:12.810
Pretending to push his 
own wall forward,

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:16.000
he pushes the two tiles into the wall.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.630
The overflowing tiles go below and 
the remaining tiles go above.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:23.560
The tiles basically mimic 
the motion of a caterpillar.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.580
The final position of the 
7 of bamboo tiles is here.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.310
His final move is to return 
two tiles from the

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:30.680
right edge in his right hand.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.240
This all happens in under two seconds.

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:36.090
There is no hesitation, as he has practiced
these motions tens of thousands of times.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:37.990
It helps that, at this point,

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:40.090
everyone is focused on 
sorting their own tiles.

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:42.260
Usually, nobody notices.

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:45.650
The two tiles he drew are not important.

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:48.930
What's important are the two 
7 of bamboo tiles he buried in his wall.

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:50.970
One is the dora indicator tile.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.680
The second tile will be the new

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:55.570
dora indicator tile when a kan is declared.

00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:58.570
Which means that in one moment,

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:02.080
Yagi can go from a trash hand to 
a monster hand with eight dora.

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:06.750
Not knowing any of this, 
Akagi, on his 12th turn...

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:07.940
Riichi.

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:10.720
<i>Gotcha!</i>

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:14.230
<i>Akagi has entrusted himself 
to the flow of the game.</i>

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:18.120
<i>Once he's sure he won't deal into anyone's 
hand and can draw his own winning tile,</i>

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:20.130
<i>he declares riichi even with his huge lead.</i>

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:22.820
<i>That hunch is probably right.</i>

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:24.500
<i>He has an innate sense for that.</i>

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:29.800
<i>But even this genius has a weak point.</i>

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:32.580
<i>He doesn't have enough 
experience with rough play.</i>

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.110
<i>And he doesn't know the 
terror of the riichi.</i>

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:37.480
<i>I'm going to teach him.</i>

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:41.960
Pon!

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:48.790
On the 13th turn, Akagi is skipped.

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:50.990
He's skipped again on the 14th turn.

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:52.390
Pon!

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:56.250
Akagi is skipped twice in a row.

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:00.990
<i>The flow doesn't mean jack 
if you can't draw a tile.</i>

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:02.700
<i>We're almost ready.</i>

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:05.710
Sorry.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.110
<i>The east wind?</i>

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:13.120
<i>The 3 of circles he put back 
should've been his draw.</i>

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:16.280
<i>Which means the guy after me 
will draw that 3 of circles.</i>

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:19.950
<i>This guy's already called twice.
Will that be his winning tile?</i>

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:22.970
<i>Cheap.</i>

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:24.320
<i>But...</i>

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:25.990
<i>it's hard to tell if that switch</i>

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:28.650
<i>was intentional or a simple mistake.</i>

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.870
<i>Even if I say something, it'll just 
turn into a pointless argument.</i>

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:35.370
Pon!

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:40.880
<i>So I guess that 3 of circles is 
going to the next player again.</i>

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:43.110
What is going on?

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:47.850
<i>It's here!</i>

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:49.850
The moment is ripe.

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:50.790
Kan!

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:55.860
<i>Four dora?</i>

00:06:58.700 --> 00:06:59.760
<i>Eight dora?!</i>

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:01.050
Riichi!

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:07.530
<i>Now do you understand 
what riichi is all about?</i>

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:10.040
<i>Once you've declared riichi, 
you're powerless to adapt</i>

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:11.680
<i>to any changes that come after.</i>

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:12.610
Chi.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:19.560
<i>So now, that 3 of circles is my draw.</i>

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:24.060
<i>It doesn't matter how suspicious you are.</i>

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:25.820
<i>Since you've already declared riichi,</i>

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:28.630
<i>you have no choice but to discard 
anything that's not your winning tile!</i>

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:35.200
A riichi can turn a genius into a peasant.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:39.000
Ron!

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:46.040
No reverse dora, but still...

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:49.470
Riichi, eight dora. Baiman.

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:49.470
Baiman - Double mangan, worth 
16,000 points (24,000 for the dealer).

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:50.760
And now I'm in the lead.

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:52.470
Akagi!

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:53.860
Don't worry.

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:55.050
These things happen.

00:07:57.170 --> 00:08:01.890
But, you know, that was quite 
the elaborate setup, Yagi-san.

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:06.310
I'm going to be more direct now.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:15.360
There's only one hand left, South Hand 4.

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:20.210
<i>He's got a slow hand.</i>

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:22.730
<i>He'd do better with a quick hand here.</i>

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:24.220
<i>A tanpin hand would be best.</i>

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:26.650
And yet, in this moment,

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:28.240
the young genius, Akagi,

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:31.220
was writing a completely 
different story in his head.

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:32.740
The second turn.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:35.350
Pon.

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:40.000
The third turn.

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:41.200
Chi.

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:46.190
<i>A quick attack.</i>

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:48.840
<i>Akagi's trying to head them off.</i>

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:52.800
<i>He's trying to make them think he's 
going for a chinitsu or honitsu hand</i>

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:52.800
Chinitsu - Full flush with only one suit.
Honitsu - Half flush including one suit and honors.

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:55.280
<i>and tying the hands of the 
other players besides Yagi.</i>

00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:59.140
<i>They don't have the balls 
to stay in the game</i>

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:01.260
<i>and risk giving him a winning 
tile in the last hand.</i>

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:04.320
<i>After he called twice, they're 
only discarding safe tiles.</i>

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:05.290
<i>They're out.</i>

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:08.790
<i>He's made this game 
between him and Yagi.</i>

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:10.790
And then, on the eighth turn...

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:12.800
Pon.

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:15.680
<i>He's called three times?</i>

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:19.040
<i>It looks like a chinitsu hand, 
but there's no way.</i>

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:21.990
<i>Those three calls are bluffs.</i>

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:23.540
<i>More like a tanyao.</i>

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:27.280
<i>With a single dora tile, 
he'll overtake my 4,000 point lead.</i>

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:31.890
<i>Damn.</i>

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:34.620
<i>I can't discard this dora.</i>

00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:36.100
<i>Damn it.</i>

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:37.990
<i>If I waste too much time, 
he'll draw his winning tile.</i>

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:43.780
However, right then, 
Akagi was in not in tenpai.

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:46.250
South Hand 4, eighth turn.

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:48.970
At this point in time, 
Akagi isn't waiting for any tiles.

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:52.570
<i>If anything, I think this is on purpose.</i>

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:57.470
<i>He's probably going for a three-way 
wait, with a 3 or 5 of characters.</i>

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:00.180
<i>He's betting on his own draws.</i>

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:03.480
<i>There is no single tile wait that 
could work in this situation.</i>

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:06.720
<i>Still, this is strange.</i>

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:11.610
<i>I feel like there's some 
hidden purpose here.</i>

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:17.560
While Yasuoka entertained his 
faint hunch, it finally happened...

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:19.430
In the 10th turn.

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:21.510
Kan.

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:25.080
<i>What?!</i>

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:26.450
<i>Why is he declaring a kan now?!</i>

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:29.740
<i>Forcing yourself to wait on 
a single tile is suicide!</i>

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:31.850
And then, Akagi...

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:40.290
He knocks over his waiting tile

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:43.490
while reaching over to 
draw from the dead wall.

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:46.260
<i>How could he make a mistake like that?</i>

00:10:49.820 --> 00:10:52.110
<i>Now he's done it.</i>

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:55.270
<i>A fatal mistake, at a time like this!</i>

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:01.110
<i>That settles it.</i>

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:02.910
<i>I know he's waiting on a characters tile.</i>

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:08.010
<i>Which means that, at least, this turn, 
any tile other than a character is safe.</i>

00:11:08.750 --> 00:11:13.560
<i>Any tile, even the extremely 
dangerous dora tile, the 4 of bamboo.</i>

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:15.160
<i>I can't believe it.</i>

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:19.250
<i>He's so calm and collected, 
and then he screws up like that!</i>

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:21.820
<i>Why did he declare a kan to begin with?</i>

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:26.630
<i>What kind of single wait 
could be worth throwing</i>

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:28.480
<i>a three-way wait away for?</i>

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:32.250
<i>Which character is he waiting for, anyway?</i>

00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:35.830
<i>What the hell?!</i>

00:11:35.830 --> 00:11:37.810
<i>There can't be five 4 of character tiles!</i>

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:40.270
<i>What?</i>

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:41.500
<i>A 4 of bamboo...</i>

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:42.690
<i>But why?</i>

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:44.300
Yasuoka is confused.

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:46.300
<i>I see.</i>

00:11:47.030 --> 00:11:50.190
<i>That 4 of characters kan was just a trick.</i>

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:54.150
<i>All Yagi sees is an ordinary kan,</i>

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:57.530
<i>but Akagi slipped the 4 of 
bamboo he had drawn into it.</i>

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:00.770
<i>And then, when he went to 
draw from the dead wall,</i>

00:12:00.770 --> 00:12:02.570
<i>he deliberately knocked it over.</i>

00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:05.840
<i>He let his opponent see 
only the character symbol,</i>

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:08.570
<i>to make him believe he was 
waiting on a character tile.</i>

00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:12.730
<i>But in reality, his wait is for a 4 of bamboo!</i>

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:15.070
<i>I can't believe it.</i>

00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:17.490
Normally, this 4 of bamboo is an

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:19.550
extremely dangerous tile 
that couldn't be discarded

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:21.750
because Akagi could be 
going for a tanyao.

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:26.190
And yet, believing that Akagi is 
actually waiting on a characters tile,

00:12:26.190 --> 00:12:27.990
it becomes the easiest 
tile to discard this turn.

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:33.240
Precisely because it is the only chance 
to get rid of the dangerous tile

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:35.000
he could not risk discarding until now.

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:52.950
You're such an idiot, Yagi-san.

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:59.670
You really believe I'd knock my tile over 
in a situation like this, even by mistake?

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:03.490
What the hell are you talking about?

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:04.650
Ron.

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:12.700
Tanyao, two dora!

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:14.900
The character tile he had glimpsed

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:17.260
had melted into the kan 
and risen to the heavens.

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:20.100
Akagi's outrageous single 
wait was for a dora tile.

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:24.210
<i>We won.</i>

00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:26.800
<i>My debt is gone.</i>

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:28.010
<i>I'm saved...</i>

00:13:28.890 --> 00:13:31.290
<i>At one point I thought I was going to die,</i>

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:35.470
<i>but Akagi saved my life.</i>

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:38.870
<i>This rough night is over.</i>

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:41.190
No.

00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:42.700
It was not over yet.

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:47.620
Right then, Akagi was thinking 
the exact opposite from Nangou.

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:50.440
Ryuuzaki-san, 
this isn't over yet, right?

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:51.510
What did you say?

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:56.170
Let's take the three million 
that erased Nangou's debt

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:58.980
and play one more match with that at stake.

00:13:59.730 --> 00:14:00.680
Double or nothing.

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:04.630
Hey, hey!

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:06.090
Don't get reckless here!

00:14:06.090 --> 00:14:07.510
If you lose, it's my life on the line!

00:14:07.510 --> 00:14:08.530
It'll be fine.

00:14:08.530 --> 00:14:10.650
Easy for you to say!

00:14:10.650 --> 00:14:12.490
You've already faced death once.

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:14.900
Let's push this to the limit.

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:17.130
But...

00:14:17.540 --> 00:14:18.550
Nangou-san.

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:20.470
You know who's feeling the

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:22.710
most pressure after the 
double or nothing proposal?

00:14:22.710 --> 00:14:23.640
They are.

00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:28.350
They're well aware that they've got 
no hopes of winning this game.

00:14:30.590 --> 00:14:33.780
Nor can they back down. 
They've got their reputation to uphold.

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:35.900
They're the kind of fools who won't back down

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:37.120
even if they know they'll lose.

00:14:37.730 --> 00:14:39.100
Sitting ducks, so to speak.

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:40.660
Akagi...

00:14:40.970 --> 00:14:43.230
We're going to milk them dry.

00:14:48.100 --> 00:14:50.940
Double or nothing. 
The wager is three million.

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:55.410
For Akagi, entering this 
game was ridiculously easy.

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:58.810
Yagi's confidence is already in shreds.

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:00.810
One more push is all it'll take.

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:02.310
Riichi.

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:07.050
Ron.

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:13.060
It was a single hell wait on a west wind.

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:13.060
Hell wait - Waiting for a tile when the other 
three are in your hand or the discard pile.

00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:16.660
Akagi instinctively knows 
how to break a person

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:19.060
who has already begun to crumble.

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:21.500
Then, in East Hand 3...

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:29.970
<i>If I discard the 3 or 6 of 
characters, I'll be in tenpai.</i>

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:32.070
<i>That's a little risky, though.</i>

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:35.910
<i>I guess I go for this, then.</i>

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:39.860
<i>That west wind from before 
left a bad taste in my mouth,</i>

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:41.220
<i>but that couldn't happen again...</i>

00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:47.220
I wasn't expecting to see that.

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:51.260
Your judgment is clouded, Yagi-san.

00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:53.250
That's it.

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:57.530
<i>A single wait for the 1 of characters?!</i>

00:15:58.170 --> 00:15:59.410
<i>That's crazy!</i>

00:15:59.410 --> 00:16:01.850
<i>If he'd used the 2 of characters 
he declared riichi with,</i>

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:03.980
<i>he could've gone for a pinfu with 
a 3 or 6 of characters wait,</i>

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:05.800
<i>or a tanyao with a 
2 or 5 of characters wait...</i>

00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:09.040
<i>He could've gotten any number 
of more expensive hands!</i>

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:11.120
<i>But he deliberately waited 
for the 1 of characters!</i>

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:16.050
<i>He's only out to get me!</i>

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:19.850
At that moment, 
something snaps in Yagi's soul.

00:16:19.850 --> 00:16:21.370
After that, he completely falls apart.

00:16:22.450 --> 00:16:25.360
The hand continues, but Yagi is 
a shadow of who he was before.

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:28.250
His play becomes thoroughly timid,

00:16:28.250 --> 00:16:30.870
perpetually ruled by Akagi's motions.

00:16:31.460 --> 00:16:33.810
He no longer has the will to fight.

00:16:34.420 --> 00:16:37.800
By making it hard to discard 
terminal and honor tiles,

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:41.010
Akagi controls Yagi's game as if 
he's laid a curse on his soul.

00:16:42.140 --> 00:16:45.140
At this time, Akagi is starting to 
grasp the fundamental principles

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:47.340
of competitive Mahjong.

00:16:48.100 --> 00:16:51.240
Mahjong is not about how 
to win your own hands.

00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:53.520
Quite the opposite.

00:16:53.950 --> 00:16:56.490
It is about how to deny 
your opponents theirs.

00:16:57.080 --> 00:17:00.530
Only those who can expand 
their own vision can win.

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:03.100
The last hand.

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:06.480
Yagi is over 50,000 points 
behind the leading Akagi,

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:08.510
but goes for a final attack.

00:17:08.510 --> 00:17:09.270
Pon!

00:17:10.670 --> 00:17:11.580
Chi!

00:17:14.150 --> 00:17:17.130
He's going for a quick attack, 
relying on his dealer streak.

00:17:14.150 --> 00:17:17.130
The dealer remains dealer as long as he wins. The last hand 
continues until someone else wins, breaking his streak.

00:17:17.650 --> 00:17:18.590
But...

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:21.170
Riichi.

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:27.020
He falls apart after Akagi's riichi.

00:17:27.320 --> 00:17:29.090
It's one safe tile after another.

00:17:31.220 --> 00:17:33.830
He called twice early on,

00:17:33.830 --> 00:17:36.020
but now his hand is a complete mess.

00:17:37.850 --> 00:17:39.310
He is falling apart.

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:44.390
After seeing Akagi's discards, he can't 
discard any terminal or honor tiles.

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:49.760
He must discard them if he wants 
to attempt a win, but he can't.

00:17:51.050 --> 00:17:54.100
And yet, he hasn't completely folded, either.

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:56.550
He's in a moment of confusion.

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:08.070
Ron.

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:21.300
It is over.

00:18:23.150 --> 00:18:25.600
I'm sorry, but I don't have 
the money on me right now.

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:27.510
I'll contact you later.

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:29.260
Come on. We're going.

00:18:33.670 --> 00:18:35.290
What are you talking about?

00:18:36.750 --> 00:18:37.640
Not yet.

00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:39.380
It's not over yet.

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:44.070
Double or nothing with the 
three million we just won.

00:18:44.590 --> 00:18:46.040
Now the game's wager is six million.

00:18:47.740 --> 00:18:49.490
I won't let you end it here.

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:52.260
We keep going to the limit,

00:18:53.250 --> 00:18:55.510
until one of us falls apart.

00:18:56.800 --> 00:18:58.790
After the match is over, 
there won't be anything left.

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:01.750
We're going until we see the depths of Hell.

00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:13.150
You're not going to run away, are you?

00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:16.910
Who's running, you jackass?

00:19:16.910 --> 00:19:20.120
I'm not running, 
but it's already morning.

00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:23.230
We'll have to do it another 
time. I don't feel like it!

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:26.140
"I can't because it's morning."

00:19:26.140 --> 00:19:27.580
Nice excuse.

00:19:27.980 --> 00:19:29.110
What?

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:30.880
There, there.

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:32.620
Leave this to me.

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:35.450
No need to get touchy.

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:37.120
Just do it on another day.

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:39.420
Think about it.

00:19:39.420 --> 00:19:42.710
Now matter how long you play 
and how much you win,

00:19:42.710 --> 00:19:45.130
there's no guarantee they'll 
be good for the money.

00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:47.440
They'll probably weasel out of it.

00:19:47.930 --> 00:19:53.740
They'll pay a few million, but not if 
it gets into the tens of millions.

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:55.640
You can bet they'll go back on their word.

00:19:56.770 --> 00:20:00.810
It would be best to officially 
hold the rematch on another day.

00:20:00.810 --> 00:20:04.620
Then I can ask a boss I know 
to supervise the match.

00:20:04.620 --> 00:20:06.880
There's a lot of money at risk here.

00:20:07.170 --> 00:20:09.220
You don't want to be reckless, Akagi.

00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:10.130
Got it?

00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:15.950
We'll stop here today, but 
I want to get this in writing.

00:20:16.580 --> 00:20:19.270
Write down that Nangou's debt is gone,

00:20:19.270 --> 00:20:21.790
and that you owe three million.

00:20:22.300 --> 00:20:25.330
And then, that you agree to a rematch.

00:20:27.720 --> 00:20:29.190
I'll arrange it.

00:20:29.540 --> 00:20:33.010
I'll contact you about the time 
and location in a few days.

00:20:33.970 --> 00:20:37.520
I'm going to provide you with 
the best stage to play on.

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:51.600
Akagi.

00:20:56.850 --> 00:20:58.990
The legendary night is over...

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:01.800
No, this is just the beginning.

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:06.050
It's the prelude to the legend 
of Akagi Shigeru, the genius

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:07.940
who descended into the darkness.

00:22:15.990 --> 00:22:18.680
Next time: "A True Outlaw."

00:22:16.910 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>Next Episode</b>

00:22:16.910 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>A True Outlaw</b>
